  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThis afternoon, GOES Channel 2 visible imagery shows dominant NW'ly \nflow across the region with minimal cloud cover atop the UP. Despite \nthe atypical status of today's insolation, with the solar eclipse \nand elevated wildfire smoke, temperatures are reaching into the high \n70s and low 80s. Weak, sprawling surface pressure underneath fairly \ndry quasi-zonal flow has resulted in calm winds, nearly normal \ntemperatures, and partly sunny skies at the surface. Some low (15-\n25%) precipitation chances enter into the forecast this evening \nalong the southeastern quadrant of the UP, and any thunderstorms \nthat form along with this are most likely to do so along the lake \nbreeze boundary. The HRRR and NAM both have lake breezes\ninitiating from Lake Superior around 20Z today. \n\nFor tomorrow, a nebulous shortwave passes through the CWA while the \nregion remains under the same synoptic pattern. CAMs are undecided \non the precise manifestations, but agree that impacts will be sub-\nsevere and mostly in the west and south of the UP. With less than 1\" \nof PWAT available tomorrow during the second half of the day, NBM 24-\nhr LPMM ending at 8 pm Aug 13 (Thurs) shows only 0.02 \n- 0.16 inches of accumulation. Otherwise, with surface high pressure \nbuilding in, temperatures are expected to remain very close to \nnormal for the next few days. \n\nThe next big chances for precipitation arrive Saturday afternoon as \nthis week's ridging exits east and a surface trough drapes down \nacross the region from Ontario. Starting with rainfall, WPC has the \nentire state of MI in a Day 4 Marginal Risk of the ERO. The EPS has \nPWAT > 1.5\" 18Z Saturday through 18Z Sunday, with greatest PWAT near \n2\" south near Menominee County; the CMCE has only 1 - 1.5\" during \nthis time. The NBM has widespread 10-20% chance of rainfall greater \nthan 0.5\", and the 90th percentiles of 24-hour accumulation \ndistributions ranges from just over 0.5\" to upwards of 1\" with the \nhighest accumulations being in the southern UP. Along with elevated \nmoisture, surface moisture appears impressive: dewpoints look to \nclimb into the 60s across the UP and into the high 60s near \nMenominee Co. Saturday evening, and remain high through Sunday. \nCorrespondingly, mean NBM SBCAPE maxes out in that same area around \n500 J/kg. Though severe weather is not expected at this time, \ndetails may change as the event moves closer.\n\nLooking further out, LREF 500 mb heights clusters start to show \ndiscrepancies at the CONUS level on Day 6 (Aug 17-18) with timing \nand behavior of Pacific trough moving onshore. These differences \nstart to be felt in Upper Midwest on Day 7 with respect to how \nquickly this late week/weekend's ridging exits our area, and inject \nuncertainty into the pattern and thus precipitation chances in the \nDay 6-7 timeframe.",
